            <ArticleTitle>NKT cells: the smoking gun in fungal-induced asthma?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1210-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nm.3360</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Aspergillus fumigatus is a fungus that is associated with a severe form of asthma, although the precise immunological basis for this disease is unclear. A new study in mice shows that natural killer T (NKT) cells are crucial for progression of A. fumigatus–induced asthma and also identifies a glycolipid antigen from this fungus that seems to drive this NKT cell–mediated inflammatory response (pages 1297–1304).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
